 ],
 "literal": "Like the cut logs of a hillman in a burnt forest clearing, / the bright flame of the funeral pyre of firewood with charred backs, / let it shrink even smaller than a heron, or without shrinking, going / to reach the sky, even if it rises high, let it rise high! / He who had a white umbrella like the cool-rayed moon, / and who was like the bright sun—his fame will never perish!",
 "translation": "Like the logs cut by a hillman in a burnt forest clearing, the bright flame of the funeral pyre rises from firewood with charred backs. Let that flame shrink to be smaller than a heron, or let it stretch without shrinking, rising high to touch the sky! The fame of him who possessed a white umbrella like the cool-rayed moon, and who was like the bright sun, will never perish!",
 "modern": "எரிக்கப்பட்ட காட்டுப் புலத்தில் குறவன் வெட்டிய மரத்துண்டுகளைப் போல, கரி படிந்த புறத்தைக் கொண்ட விறகுகளால் எரியும் ஈமத்தின் ஒளிரும் நெருப்பே! நீ குருகை விடவும் சிறிதாகச் சுருங்கிப் போனாலும் போகுக! அல்லது சுருங்காமல் மேலே சென்று வானத்தைத் தொடுமளவிற்கு உயர்ந்து நீண்டாலும் நீளுக! குளிர்ந்த கதிர்களை உடைய திங்களைப் போன்ற வெண்கொற்றக் குடையையும், ஒளிரும் ஞாயிற்றைப் போன்ற ஆற்றலையும் உடைய அவனது புகழ் என்றும் அழியாது!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a moving lament (kaiyaṟunilai) by the famous poet Avvaiyar on the death of her patron, the chieftain Adhiyaman Neduman Anji. Standing before his funeral pyre, she addresses the bright flame consuming his body. She compares the firewood of the pyre, with its charred backs, to the logs cut by a hillman in a slash-and-burn forest clearing. Avvaiyar defiantly tells the fire that it may shrink to be smaller than a heron or leap up high to touch the sky; it makes no difference because the fire can only consume his physical body. The fame of Adhiyaman, who ruled with a cool, moon-like white umbrella of justice and shone like the bright sun in battle, is immortal and will never perish.",
